{:edb/db-before-t 2463 :edb/db-after-t 2464 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535776 50 #inst "2026-10-09T07:03:05.825Z" 13194139535776 true] [17592186198004 1100 "Determine the quadratic Taylor polynomial for the function of two variables\n$f(x,y) = \\cos(x+y)$\nabout the point $(0,0)$." 13194139535776 false] [17592186198004 1100 "Determine the quadratic Taylor polynomial for the function of two variables \n$f(x,y) = \\cos(x+y)$\nabout the point $(0,0).$" 13194139535776 true] [17592186198004 1145 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (0,0)\n$$\n\nthen the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x, y) \\mid \\approx f(0, 0) + f_{x}(0, 0) \\cdot x + f_{y}(0, 0) \\cdot y^(⏞)^(Linear approximation); \\approx + \\frac{1}{2}(f_{xx}(0, 0) \\cdot x^{2} + 2f_{xy}(0, 0) \\cdot xy + f_{yy}(0, 0) \\cdot y^{2})_(⏟)_(Quadratic part)\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n$f(0, 0)|= 1$.\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}f_{x}(x, y)|=-\\sin (x + y), & f_{y}(x, y)|=-\\sin (x + y) \\\\ f_{x}(0, 0)|= 0, & f_{y}(0, 0)|= 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}f_{xx}(x, y)|=-\\cos (x + y), & f_{xx}(0, 0)|=-1 \\\\ f_{xy}(x, y)|=-\\cos (x + y), & f_{xy}(0, 0)|=-1 \\\\ f_{yy}(x, y)|=-\\cos (x + y), & f_{yy}(0, 0)|=-1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x, y) \\mid \\approx 1 + 0 \\cdot x + 0 \\cdot y + \\frac{1}{2}((-1) \\cdot x^{2} + 2(-1) \\cdot xy + (-1) \\cdot y^{2}); = 1 - \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} - xy - \\frac{1}{2}y^{2}\n$$" 13194139535776 false] [17592186198004 1145 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y) = (0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}((-1)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-1)\\cdot xy+(-1)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-xy-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535776 true] [17592186198005 1100 "For the function $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$, defined as\n\n$f\\left(x,y \\right) = x^2y-xy^3$,\n\ndetermine the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Give your answer in matrix form." 13194139535776 false] [17592186198005 1100 "For the function $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf\\left(x,y \\right) \n=\nx^2y-xy^3,\n$$\n\ndetermine the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/b799b1e1fbe7819de86c131989530033d750669ddc8fee88c711b537dad982a6.png) Give your answer in matrix form." 13194139535776 true] [17592186198005 1145 "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand is twice differentiable at\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\n$$\n\nThen, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\mid \\approx f(a) + f^{′}(a)(x - a) + \\frac{1}{2}(x - a)^{T}f^{″}(a)(x - a)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, \\mid f^{″}(x)|= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n$f(1, 1)|= 0$.\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n$f^{′}(x)|= \\begin{bmatrix}2xy - y^{3} & x^{2} - 3xy^{2}\\end{bmatrix}; f^{′}(1, 1)|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n$f^{″}(x)|= \\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2x - 3y^{2} \\\\ 2x - 3y^{2} & -6xy\\end{bmatrix}; f^{″}(1, 1)|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial (in matrix form) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535776 false] [17592186198005 1145 "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/b1c2d4408c64daf16466d81c1ea97bdd32f821deb858cd09bc539fc53ebf3ad8.png) \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/c54318fa746f53209585695e2c40d80d793d2b35327b257426b4e4fa7b25463b.png)\n\nand\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/952dafd201f20f1da5b0ac77527beeb4f6d7c259813dbaee74a8d6796dd5d3b2.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2xy-{y}^{3} & {x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x-3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-3{y}^{2} & -6xy\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial (in matrix form) is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/fc105c91cf998f6b564570697321cfb72ac30f81e88dd6c1a0decfe8495259ba.png)" 13194139535776 true] [17592186198006 1100 "For the twice-differentiable function $f:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R$, we have the following data:\n\n$f(1, 0, 0)|=-1, \\mid f^{′}(1, 0, 0)|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\mid f^{″}(1, 0, 0)|= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(1,1,1)$." 13194139535776 false] [17592186198006 1100 "For the twice-differentiable function $f:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,0) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/32af7a7ab867c247eff15583688e31052e2d640b1be6c6c1b1f8bd19b7b7f2f7.png) to approximate the value of $f(1,1,1).$" 13194139535776 true] [17592186198006 1145 "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand is twice differentiable at\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\n$$\n\nThen, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\mid \\approx f(a) + f^{′}(a)(x - a) + \\frac{1}{2}(x - a)^{T}f^{″}(a)(x - a)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(a) &= -1, \\mid f^{′}(a)|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\mid f^{″}(a)|= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf(1, 1, 1) \\mid \\approx - 1 + \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 - 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 - 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 - 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}; =-1 + \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}; =-1 + 1 + \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}; = \\frac{1}{2}(3); = \\frac{3}{2}\n$$" 13194139535776 false] [17592186198006 1145 "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/f27bcd29776525aa543b778627e0aace4e0b0537ceac83f5750a31f5def4f762.png) \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/c281563d780c34ea5753498e0e722bfb0296fe06c5d43e82473a45901a1aa7ee.png)\n\nand\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/2fa9abbaad8230a38e1085b8842fbbd970c66addff6f388e13973ee116f0cd37.png)\n\nIn this case, we have ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/71175744ceb56efc01b8b18b97d6681b11b8e9b85a1d899fc9a137ab5e3c611c.png) and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/0d3a22cd82a02ab5122e811236d53eb4c0018e9817d2542163acc005ca5d3764.png)\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/dad24b303457c56dc14c11b3b9de6cb827b18b8f3a03059922d1b15521ea119d.png) we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,1) & ≈-1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+1+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535776 true] [17592186198361 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365647 13194139535